Subject: Very Important: Broker grouping and listener isolation in kafka clusters
To: users@kafka.apache.org
Content-Type: multipart/alternative; boundary="000000000000b5b9520652a0f1ef"
From: sunil chaudhari <sunilmchaudhari05@gmail.com>
Reply-To: <users@kafka.apache.org>

--000000000000b5b9520652a0f1ef
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable

Hello Kafka users,
This is claude response for the problem mentioned.
can some expert comment on this and suggest better approach?

*Problem*:

I have 30 kafka brokers in my prod clusters. I want to group brokers based
on the producer country. For example singapore will connext on 9092 port,
India will connect on 9094 port and so on. However i have to add both port
listener on all brokers. Hence I cant achive any benefit from DR point of
view of migration point of view. Exmaple I want to trigger DR only for
singapore and switch it off. I cant simply bring down 9092. I have to
change the config and restart all brokers. What is the solution of this
problem?


*Response from Claude: *
This is a classic listener isolation vs. broker grouping problem. The root
cause is that Kafka=E2=80=99s listener model is per-broker, not
per-group-of-brokers.

The Real Solution: Broker Groups via Rack Awareness + Dedicated Listener
Brokers

Instead of adding all listeners to all brokers, you need to dedicate a
subset of brokers per producer country/region.

Architecture: Listener-Segregated Broker Groups

30 Brokers split into logical groups:

Group A (Brokers 1=E2=80=9310) =E2=86=92 Singapore producers =E2=86=92 LIST=
ENER: SG_LISTENER:9092
Group B (Brokers 11=E2=80=9320) =E2=86=92 India producers =E2=86=92 LISTENE=
R: IN_LISTENER:9094
Group C (Brokers 21=E2=80=9330) =E2=86=92 Other/Internal =E2=86=92 LISTENER=
: INT_LISTENER:9096


Each broker group only has its own listener, not all listeners.

Kafka Config per Group

Group A (Singapore brokers):

listeners=3DSG_LISTENER://0.0.0.0:9092
advertised.listeners=3DSG_LISTENER://broker-sg-01.internal:9092
listener.security.protocol.map=3DSG_LISTENER:SASL_SSL
broker.rack=3Dsg-az1


Group B (India brokers):

listeners=3DIN_LISTENER://0.0.0.0:9094
advertised.listeners=3DIN_LISTENER://broker-in-01.internal:9094
listener.security.protocol.map=3DIN_LISTENER:SASL_SSL
broker.rack=3Din-az1


DR Trigger Mechanism

Now when you need to trigger DR for Singapore:



|Action |What you do |
|-------------------------|------------------------------------------------=
--------------------|
|DR cutover |Update DNS / load balancer to stop routing SG producers to
port 9092|
|Broker isolation |Shut down brokers 1=E2=80=9310 only, others unaffected |
|No rolling restart needed|India brokers (11=E2=80=9320) never had 9092 =E2=
=80=94 nothing
to change |
|Re-onboard after DR |Bring Group A back, DNS flips back |

The Missing Piece: Partition Assignment via Rack Awareness

Use broker.rack + replica.selector.class so Singapore topic partitions are
leader-preferenced on Group A brokers:

# On all brokers
replica.selector.class=3Dorg.apache.kafka.common.replica.RackAwareReplicaSe=
lector


When creating topics, set --replica-assignment explicitly or use a custom
AdminClient script to pin partition leaders to the correct rack group.

What About the Bootstrap Problem?

Producers need to know which brokers to bootstrap from. Use separate
bootstrap endpoints per region:

sg-kafka.internal:9092 =E2=86=92 resolves to brokers 1=E2=80=9310 only (via=
 DNS/LB)
in-kafka.internal:9094 =E2=86=92 resolves to brokers 11=E2=80=9320 only


This way your bootstrap.servers in producer config is region-aware, and the
listener port difference reinforces the segregation.

Summary of What Changes vs Your Current Design

|Problem you had |Solution |
|---------------------------------|----------------------------------------=
----------------------|
|All listeners on all brokers |Assign listeners per broker group only |
|DR requires restart of all 30 |DR only touches the 10 brokers in that
group |
|Can=E2=80=99t bring down one port cleanly|Bringing down Group A brokers =
=3D clean SG
DR, others unaffected|
|Migration risk is cluster-wide |Changes scoped to one group of 10 |

The key insight is: the listener boundary should match the broker group
boundary, not span the whole cluster.

